# ===== Kern self-host configuration =====
# Public URL users open in the browser (Caddy terminates TLS automatically for real domains)
KERN_DOMAIN=kern.example.com
KERN_BASE_URL=https://kern.example.com
# Use "internal" for a self-signed cert on LAN/IP installs, or an email for Let's Encrypt
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# Image tag for every Kern service. install.sh resolves the newest stable release and writes the
# number here, and kern-upgrade.sh rewrites it — this should always be a concrete version.
#
# Do not set it back to `latest`. Rollback is recorded as "the version we came from", so an
# instance on `latest` snapshots `from-version: latest` and kern-rollback.sh would re-pin the
# version you just moved to: a rollback that reports success and changes nothing. `latest` also
# lets a pull that lands mid-release give five services five different builds, which is the drift
# docs/adr/0002-platform-versioning-and-updates.md exists to prevent.
KERN_VERSION=latest

# The shell and core images. Leave these as they are to run what Kern publishes. Point them at
# your own registry when you have built the pair with modules of your own baked in
# (`--build-arg KERN_EXTRA_MODULES=…`, see docs.kernaio.com/developers/module-development/).
# Both must be built from the same Kern version, and that version is still KERN_VERSION.
KERN_IMAGE_SHELL=ghcr.io/kernaio/shell
KERN_IMAGE_CORE=ghcr.io/kernaio/core

# Where the web app reaches core from inside the compose network, for the pages it renders itself
# (published Quire pages). This is not a browser address: leave it as the service name. Pointing it
# at your public domain makes the app container leave the network and come back in through Caddy to
# reach a service one hop away — and on an IP or LAN install, where Caddy issues its own certificate,
# Node rejects that certificate and every published page fails to render.
KERN_API_ORIGIN=http://core:4000

# Absolute path of this directory, written by install.sh. Only the optional `updater` container
# reads it: it runs docker compose against the host's daemon, so the compose file's relative bind
# mounts have to resolve to the same path inside the container and out.
KERN_DIR=

# Time zone the optional updater container reads the update window in. The window and its zone are
# set in the interface (Admin -> Updates); this only affects the container's own clock.
TZ=UTC

# Secrets — install.sh generates these. Keep them hex or alphanumeric: KERN_DB_APP_PASSWORD goes
# into DATABASE_URL, where a `@`, `:`, `/` or `#` would be parsed as URL syntax.
KERN_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
KERN_DB_APP_PASSWORD=
S3_SECRET_KEY=

# Video calls are NOT built, and these two are here only because docker-compose.yml still carries the
# `calls` profile. No Kern service reads LIVEKIT_URL, LIVEKIT_API_KEY or LIVEKIT_API_SECRET; there is
# no calls module. Nothing here needs your attention.
#
# install.sh used to offer "Enable video calls (LiveKit)?" and generate a key pair for them. Saying
# yes started a LiveKit server publishing 7881/tcp and 50000-50200/udp — 201 ports an operator then
# opened on their firewall — and nothing ever connected to it. The offer is gone; the profile stays
# so that whatever builds calls has the shape to start from, and livekit.yaml now asks for two ports
# rather than 201: 7882/udp for media and 7881/tcp as the fallback.
#
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY is an identifier, not a secret. It names which of a LiveKit server's key pairs
# signed a token and travels in the clear inside every one of them, so it is fixed rather than
# generated — a fixed name is what livekit.yaml can refer to, and Compose does not interpolate into
# a file it mounts. LIVEKIT_API_SECRET is the secret half; it stays empty until something reads it.
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=kern
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
# Shared secret your email provider's bounce and complaint webhooks must present, as `?token=` or an
# `x-kern-webhook-token` header. Point the provider at
# https://your-domain/api/mail/webhooks/<provider>?token=<this value>.
#
# `webhooks`, plural — that is the route mail actually serves. The singular this line carried until
# 2026-09-05 was a URL that 404s, and a 404 on a bounce webhook is silent: the provider records a
# failed delivery attempt of its own and Kern never marks the address bounced.
#
# It is not optional. A bounce webhook that anyone can post to lets a stranger permanently suppress
# any address on the instance, so mail refuses every webhook while this is empty — bounces stop being
# recorded, which is the safe direction to fail in.
MAIL_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# Postgres. POSTGRES_USER is the bootstrap superuser and is used for administration only —
# migrations, backups, and the db-init service. The services themselves connect as `kern_app`,
# which db-init creates NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS, because a superuser bypasses row-level security
# unconditionally and would make every tenant-isolation policy in every module do nothing.
POSTGRES_USER=kern
POSTGRES_DB=kern

# Object storage (bundled MinIO by default; point to any S3 for external)
#
# S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT is the bare origin, with no path of its own. Presigned URLs are SigV4
# signatures over the canonical path, so MinIO has to receive the byte-identical path core signed;
# Caddy routes /<bucket>/* straight through without stripping anything. Giving this a `/s3` prefix
# is what made every upload and download fail with 403 SignatureDoesNotMatch.
#
# S3_BUCKET therefore has to stay `kern` while you use the bundled MinIO — it is the Caddy route,
# and it is reserved in core so no workspace can shadow it. An external S3 is reached directly and
# has no such constraint.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=https://kern.example.com
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=kern
S3_ACCESS_KEY=kern

# Instance default outbound email (workspaces can override with their own provider)
SMTP_URL=
MAIL_FROM="Kern <no-reply@kern.example.com>"

# Optional: first admin (created on first boot if set)
KERN_ADMIN_EMAIL=
KERN_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Who may create an account: "open" for anyone, "invite" for an invitation or an administrator only.
# This seeds the setting on the very first boot and is ignored afterwards — once the instance exists,
# Admin -> Settings owns it and editing this line changes nothing. Leave it commented out and an
# instance with the admin above configured starts invite-only, which is the right default for a
# server reachable from the internet.
#
# Commented out rather than left blank, and that is not a style choice. `KERN_SIGNUP=` sets the
# variable to the empty string, and core parses it with `z.enum(['open','invite']).optional()`, which
# rejects '' as "Invalid option" instead of reading it as absent — so core threw at boot, and
# core-worker, chat, mail and collab all wait on core being healthy. A blank line here is a stack
# that never comes up. Uncomment it only to set a real value.
#KERN_SIGNUP=open

# ===== Sign in with Google, GitHub or Microsoft (optional) =====
# Kern ships social sign-in in every image; it is off until you fill this section in. Passwords,
# magic links and passkeys keep working either way, so leaving it empty is the normal install.
#
# Two things have to agree, and configuring only the first is the mistake worth naming: the
# credentials below make a provider *work*, and PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDERS is what puts its button on the
# sign-in and sign-up screens. The web app cannot ask core which providers are configured, so a
# provider missing from that list has no button however complete its credentials are — and a provider
# in the list without credentials draws a button that fails when it is pressed.
#
# Every line here is commented out on purpose. `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=` is a value — the empty string —
# not an absence, and a blank assignment defeats the pass-through in docker-compose.yml. Uncomment a
# pair only when you have both halves of it.
#
# Where to get them, and the redirect URI to register with each (replace kern.example.com with your
# KERN_DOMAIN — the path is the same in all three):
#
#   Google     console.cloud.google.com -> APIs & Services -> Credentials -> OAuth client ID (Web)
#              https://kern.example.com/api/auth/callback/google
#   GitHub     github.com/settings/developers -> OAuth Apps -> New OAuth App
#              https://kern.example.com/api/auth/callback/github
#   Microsoft  portal.azure.com -> Microsoft Entra ID -> App registrations -> New registration
#              https://kern.example.com/api/auth/callback/microsoft
#
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
#GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
#MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
#MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Leave unset to accept any Microsoft account; set a directory id to admit one tenant only.
#MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=
#
# The buttons to draw, in order: a comma-separated subset of google,github,microsoft.
#PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDERS=google,github

# ===== Billing (optional) =====
# Kern ships a billing module in every image. It does nothing until you both define plans, in
# Admin -> Plans, and set a Stripe key here — so leaving this section empty is the normal
# self-hosted install: unlimited seats, unlimited storage, every module, no payment.
#
# Fill it in if you want to run your own Kern as a service and charge for workspaces on it.
# Use test keys (sk_test_...) until you are ready to take real money.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# From `stripe listen` locally, or the endpoint you create in the Stripe dashboard pointing at
# https://your-domain/api/billing/webhook
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Slug of the plan a newly created workspace starts on. Empty means no plan and no limits.
KERN_DEFAULT_PLAN_SLUG=
